<p>Unit 1 introduced the idea that the arts reveal values. Unit 2 takes on a harder question: what actually qualifies as a work of art in the first place? This turns out to be surprisingly difficult to answer. There is no definition that everyone agrees on, and the boundaries keep moving.</p>
<p>This unit gives you a framework for thinking about that question. It introduces the distinction between identifying art conceptually (by criteria like authorship, intention, and expert agreement) and identifying art perceptually (by looking at what the object actually does to you). Along the way, you will learn four terms that will follow you through the rest of the course: artistic form, participation, subject matter, and content. These are the basic tools for talking about what a work of art is and how it works.</p>
<p>Pay special attention to the concept of participation. It describes something you have almost certainly experienced but may never have had a word for: the moment when a work of art holds your attention so fully that you lose awareness of yourself as a separate observer. That experience is at the center of everything this course does.</p>

<div class="callout callout--note"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">ℹ️</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>Learning Objectives</p>
<p>After completing this unit, you should be able to:</p>
<ul>
<li>Explain the three criteria for identifying a work of art conceptually</li>
<li>Distinguish between perception and conception</li>
<li>Define artistic form, participation, subject matter, and content</li>
<li>Explain how artistic form creates unity in a work of art</li>
<li>Distinguish between subject matter and content, and explain how form connects them</li>
</ul>




